  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coordinated approach for Land Restoration in Vulnerable Ecosystems of Central Asia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">E.2 Socio-economic Benefits and Decent Rural Employment [57]  169. This project aims to deliver significant socio-economic benefits at regional, national, and local levels. At the regional level the project will strengthen policy coordination and dialogue among the riparian countries with the goal to enhance environmental and social sustainability by promoting the harmonious integration of water and land management practices. At the national level, the project will promote ecosystem services and social society participation in planning and decision-making,  which will further support the country&apos;s commitment to biodiversity conservation and climate resilience.   170. Additionally, the project will have tangible socio-economic impacts at the local level, particularly for rural communities where agricultural productivity and livelihoods are threatened by unsustainable practices and environmental degradation. By supporting investments in sustainable land and water management and strengthening community-based resource management, the project will create job opportunities and generate income for local populations. Additionally, promoting biodiversity-friendly practices and enhancing resilience in agricultural systems will ensure food security and economic stability for rural households.  171. Social benefits include the empowerment of local communities through the involvement in water-land nexus initiatives and processes, enhanceing the understanding of the importance of ecosystem services and biodiversity for the agriculture sector and its sustainability. Inclusive and participatory approaches will foster equitable distribution of the benefits derived from sustainable resource management, promoting social cohesion. Resilience and food security will be enhanced for vulnerable smallholders.  172. Furthermore, in project areas, the project will actively promote decent work and productive employment in rural areas, aligning with international labor principles. Through capacity-building activities, training programs, and the dissemination of best practices, the project will empower local communities to engage in sustainable agriculture and natural resource management practices, enhancing their livelihoods and overall well-being. Project actions align with Pillar 1 of FAO&#x2019;s Decent Work Agenda [58], supporting employment creation and enterprise development. By fostering inclusive and participatory approaches, the project will ensure equitable distribution of the benefits derived from sustainable resource management, promoting social cohesion and economic development.          [56] Global Environment Facility (2024). Strategy for Knowledge Management and Learning. Available at https://www.thegef.org/newsroom/publications/strategy-knowledge-management-and-learning       [57] Specific guidance on how FAO can promote the Four Pillars of Decent Work in rural areas is provided in the Quick reference for addressing decent rural employment (as well as in the full corresponding Guidance document). For more information on FAO&#x2019;s work on decent rural employment and related guidance materials please consult the FAO thematic website at: http://www.fao.org/rural-employment/en/.      [58] Specific guidance on how FAO can promote the Four Pillars of Decent Work in rural areas is provided in the Quick reference for addressing decent rural employment (as well as in the full corresponding Guidance document). For more information on FAO&#x2019;s work on decent rural employment and related guidance materials please consult the FAO thematic website at: http://www.fao.org/rural-employment/en/.</narrative>
  </description>
